data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

The EF Core Cosmos DB provider **removes the sync-over-async fallback entirely**. In .NET 10 you could opt in via `EnableSyncOverAsync()`, but in .NET 11 that API is gone. Every synchronous LINQ call (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`) and `SaveChanges()` on the Cosmos provider now throws an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

## Why

The Cosmos DB SDK is async-only under the hood. The sync shim caused thread-pool starvation and deadlocks, so it was removed.

## Fix — convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- Return types → `Task<T>` / `Task`
- Methods → `async` + `Async` suffix
- LINQ terminals → `ToListAsync()`, `FirstAsync()`, `CountAsync()`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`

Callers must also be updated to `await` these methods, which typically means propagating `async` up the call chain through controllers/services.
